Over the last 3 years, she has developed intermittent abdominal cramping, diarrhea, nausea, and unexplained fatigue. Examination reveals multiple small, dark-red to blue-black, non-blanching vascular lesions concentrated around the lower abdomen, hips, thighs, and genital region. Ophthalmologic examination demonstrates a subtle bilateral whorl-like corneal opacity.

Urinalysis shows persistent low-grade proteinuria despite normal serum creatinine. ECG demonstrates increased left ventricular voltage, and echocardiography reveals mild concentric left ventricular hypertrophy. Her younger brother has similar episodes of severe burning pain and heat intolerance.

Neurologic examination demonstrates impaired temperature sensation in the distal extremities. Quantitative sensory testing is abnormal, and skin biopsy demonstrates reduced intraepidermal nerve-fiber density. A metabolic evaluation reveals markedly reduced activity of a lysosomal enzyme, with elevated plasma globotriaosylsphingosine. Molecular testing of the corresponding X-linked gene is requested.
