Neurologic examination demonstrates impaired visually enhanced vestibulo-ocular reflexes, gait ataxia, and reduced vibration sensation in both feet. Nerve conduction studies demonstrate a sensory neuronopathy with markedly reduced or absent sensory action potentials, while motor conduction is relatively preserved. Video head-impulse testing demonstrates bilateral vestibular hypofunction. Cerebellar examination reveals mild dysarthria and impaired tandem gait.

He reports a persistent dry cough that began approximately 12 years ago, long before his balance problems became noticeable. There is no significant limb weakness. Brain MRI shows mild cerebellar atrophy without inflammatory lesions. Routine autoimmune, infectious, and metabolic investigations are unrevealing. His parents are unaffected, and there is no obvious family history.

Genetic testing for repeat expansions associated with adult-onset sensory ataxia and vestibular dysfunction is requested.

