28-year-old male, previously well, presented with high-grade intermittent fever for 5 days. Fever associated with severe bifrontal headache, generalized body aches, nausea and poor oral intake. Since yesterday evening, family members have noticed that he is more irritable than usual and has had episodes of confusion, including giving inappropriate answers to simple questions. No witnessed seizure, loss of consciousness, focal limb weakness, slurred speech or recent head injury.

Patient returned 10 days ago from a 9-day trip to a rural area of southern India. During the trip he stayed in temporary accommodation and spent several evenings outdoors. Reports multiple mosquito bites. No known sick contacts. No recent animal bite. No recent freshwater swimming. No known chronic medical illness. Not taking regular medication. No known drug allergies.

On arrival, temperature 39.3°C, pulse 110/min, BP 106/68 mmHg, respiratory rate 20/min and SpO₂ 98% on room air. Patient appears febrile and mildly dehydrated. GCS 14/15. Awake and able to follow commands but intermittently confused. Complains of severe headache and mild photophobia. Neck stiffness present. Pupils equal and reactive. Extraocular movements intact. Facial movements symmetrical. Speech clear. Power 5/5 in all four limbs with no obvious sensory deficit. No pronator drift. No focal neurological deficit identified. Chest clear bilaterally. Heart sounds normal. Abdomen soft and non-tender. No obvious petechial or purpuric rash.

Initial blood tests: WBC 8.7 ×10⁹/L, hemoglobin 14.2 g/dL, platelets 138 ×10⁹/L, CRP elevated. Creatinine within normal range. AST and ALT mildly elevated. Blood cultures sent. Malaria testing sent. Dengue testing sent. Further infectious work-up planned according to clinical findings and exposure history.

Patient remains febrile with persistent headache and intermittent confusion. Neurological observations started. Brain imaging requested. CSF examination to be considered after assessment for safety and contraindications.

