omplaints & History of Present Illness\n**Chief Complaint:** Arthralgia, Fever.\nPatient presents for evaluation of arthralgia, fever, retro-orbital pain, pruritic rash, low-grade fever, non-purulent conjunctivitis, conjunctivitis, rash. Associated symptoms include retro-orbital pain, pruritic rash, low-grade fever, non-purulent conjunctivitis, conjunctivitis, rash. [PATIENT_NAME_REDACTED] Indonesia (Indonesia) with low-grade fever, maculopapular pruritic rash, non-purulent conjunctivitis, arthralgia, retro-orbital pain. Suspected exposure to Active Transmission in Indonesia (Per US CDC Surveillance). Hallmark presentation: zika, aedes, congenital microcephaly.\n\n### Relevant Clinical History\n**Past Medical History:** No documented chronic medical illnesses.\n**Current Medications:** No regular daily outpatient prescription medications reported.\n**Allergies:** No Known Drug Allergies (NKDA).\n**Social & Travel Exposure:** Non-smoker, denies illicit substances. Social history non-contributory.\n**Review of Systems:**\n\u2022 Constitutional: Positive for fever\n\u2022 Dermatological: Positive for rash\n\n### Examination & Vital Signs\n**Vital Signs:** BP: 120/80 mmHg | HR: 76 bpm | Temp: 37.0\u00b0C | SpO2: 98% | RR: 16/min (Standard Range)\n**Physical Examination:**\nGeneral: Alert, oriented x3, in no acute distress.\nCardiovascular: S1/S2 present, regular rhythm, no murmurs.\nRespiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally, no wheezes or crackles.\nAbdomen: Soft, non-tender, non-distended, active bowel sounds.\n\n### Diagnostic Investigations\n* **Dengue NS1 Antigen** [ORDERED] (Pending) \u2014 Pending\n* **Dengue IgM/IgG Serology** [ORDERED] (Pending) \u2014 Pending\n* **Complete Blood Count** [ORDERED] (Pending) \u2014 Pending\n\n### Clinical Encounter Timeline (Chronological)\n**05 Oct 2026, 04:51 PM | Arrival & Triage \u2014 Emergency Department / Patient Arrival**\n* Finding: Patient presented with: Arthralgia, Fever.. Triage Vitals: Vitals recorded at bedside.\n* Action: Triage vitals recorded; IV access established; bed assigned in Acute Care.\n* Response: Patient stabilized for physician evaluation.\n\n**05 Oct 2026, 04:51 PM | History & Examination \u2014 Initial History & Systemic Physical Examination**\n* Finding: Presenting symptoms documented: Acute symptoms documented. Social/exposure history reviewed.\n* Action: Comprehensive physical and neurological examination documented by attending clinician.\n* Response: Initial clinical impression formulated.\n\n**05 Oct 2026, 04:51 PM | Initial Assessment \u2014 AI Differential Synthesized (v1) \u2014 Decision Support**\n* Finding: Considered diagnoses: Dengue Fever (98% Match), Measles (98% Match), Zika Virus (96% Match). Primary consideration: Dengue Fever.\n* Action: Targeted diagnostic workup formulated based on clinical criteria and incubation fit.\n* Response: Orders prepared for laboratory and diagnostic imaging.\n\n**05 Oct 2026, 04:51 PM | Investigations Ordered \u2014 Diagnostic Investigations Dispatched (3 tests)**\n* Finding: Orders: Dengue NS1 Antigen, Dengue IgM/IgG Serology, Complete Blood Count.\n* Action: Specimens collected under aseptic precautions and requisitions transmitted.\n* Response: Awaiting laboratory processing and imaging execution.\n\n**05 Oct 2026, 04:51 PM | Treatment & Management \u2014 Empiric Management & Supportive Care Initiated**\n* Finding: Initial management directed towards acute presentation: Paracetamol 500-1000mg q6h PRN (max 3g/day).\n* Action: Administered IV fluids, symptomatic antipyretics, and targeted supportive measures.\n* Response: Tolerating medical therapy; continuous bedside monitoring active.\n\n**05 Oct 2026, 04:51 PM | Clinical Reassessment \u2014 Encounter Reassessment & Monitoring**\n* Finding: Hemodynamic parameters and neurological status monitored.\n* Action: Supportive care maintained; pending investigations tracked.\n* Response: Patient under continuous inpatient clinical care.\n\n**05 Oct 2026, 04:51 PM | Disposition \u2014 Clinician Disposition & Plan**\n* Finding: Inpatient care indicated for completion of diagnostic workup and clinical monitoring.\n* Action: Admitted to Ward / Unit; scheduled follow-up and safety net instructions established.\n* Response: Clinician authenticated.\n\n### Differential Diagnoses Considered (AI Decision Support)\n> *REFERENCE INFORMATION \u2014 CLINICIAN REVIEW REQUIRED \u2014 NOT A CONFIRMED DIAGNOSIS*\n\n1. **Dengue Fever** (Primary Consideration \u2014 98% Match)\n   * Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; 5 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   * Supporting evidence: fever, joint pain, pruritic rash, rash, retro-orbital headache\n   * Contradicting / absent: severe headache\n   * Recommended confirmation/exclusion: Dengue NS1 Antigen (days 1-5), Dengue IgM/IgG Serology (day 5+), Complete Blood Count (Serial Platelets and Hematocrit), Dengue NS1 Antigen and IgM/IgG ELISA, Serial CBC with Platelet Count\n\n2. **Measles** (Secondary Differential \u2014 98% Match)\n   * Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; 4 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   * Supporting evidence: fever, pruritic rash, rash, redness of the eyes\n   * Contradicting / absent: cough, mucosal lesion\n   * Recommended confirmation/exclusion: Measles Virus RT-PCR (Throat swab, nasopharyngeal swab, or urine), Measles Specific IgM Antibody Capture ELISA (positive from day 3 of rash), Oral Cavity Examination (detection of Koplik spots on buccal mucosa opposite molars), Measles RT-PCR (most sensitive within first 3 days of rash), Serum Measles IgM and IgG (paired serology showing 4-fold IgG rise)\n\n3. **Zika Virus** (Secondary Differential \u2014 96% Match)\n   * Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; 6 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   * Supporting evidence: fever, joint pain, pruritic rash, rash, redness of the eyes, retro-orbital headache\n   * Contradicting / absent: None prominent\n   * Recommended confirmation/exclusion: Zika Virus RT-PCR (Serum & Urine), Zika IgM ELISA, Dengue and Chikungunya PCR/Serology, Zika Virus RT-PCR (serum <=7 days, urine <=14 days), Zika Virus IgM ELISA\n\n4. **Varicella (Chickenpox)** (Rule Out Consideration \u2014 84% Match)\n   * Why considered: 4 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   * Supporting evidence: fever, pruritic rash, rash, retro-orbital headache\n   * Contradicting / absent: skin lesion\n   * Recommended confirmation/exclusion: Complete Blood Count (CBC) with Differential, Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (Electrolytes, Renal Function, LFTs), High-Sensitivity C-Reactive Protein (hs-CRP) and ESR, Urinalysis with Microscopic Examination, 12-Lead Electrocardiogram (ECG)\n\n5. **Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)** (Rule Out Consideration \u2014 83% Match)\n   * Why considered: 3 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   * Supporting evidence: fever, joint pain, rash\n   * Contradicting / absent: positive antinuclear antibody, hypocomplementemia, malar rash, positive anti-dsdna\n   * Recommended confirmation/exclusion: Antinuclear Antibody (ANA) by IFA, Anti-double-stranded DNA (anti-dsDNA), Anti-Smith (anti-Sm) Antibody, ANA by immunofluorescence (>98% sensitive), Anti-dsDNA (titers correlate with lupus nephritis activity)\n\n\n### Assessment & Plan\n**Clinician Assessment:** Under active clinical evaluation\n**Management Plan:**\nSupportive therapy and monitoring\n**Clinical Response / Hospital Course:** Patient admitted for inpatient clinical evaluation; response to supportive medical therapy and hydration actively monitored.\n**Disposition:** Inpatient Admission to Department of General Medicine / Acute Medical Unit.\nCondition at disposition: Clinically stable under continuous observation.\n**Follow-Up:** Follow up in outpatient clinic in 3 to 5 days, or sooner if symptoms escalate or fail to improve.\n**Safety Net Precautions:** Seek emergency medical evaluation immediately if experiencing severe chest pain, shortness of breath, persistent high fever >102\u00b0F, altered consciousness, intractable vomiting, or inability to tolerate oral fluids.\n\n### Clinician Authentication\n**Prepared by:** Department of General Medicine (General Medicine)\n**Date:** 06 October 2026 \u2014 11:57 AM\n**Authentication:** Electronically signed and verified by Attending Physician. DocAssistIQ Clinical Decision Support Record."}}', 'draft', 1, true, '268ed636-bf30-4414-b449-66e7a9cdfa3e', '2026-10-06 11:57:51.351776', '2026-10-06 11:57:51.351776', NULL, 'fa9e2981-e8cc-4a40-90a5-bbd5fb2303ae');
