ary Differential \u2014 98% Match)\n   Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; endemic in visited countries (0.30 geographic score); incubation period fits (10 days); 5 symptoms match; syndrome: neurological_fever\n   Supporting: fever, headache, intermittent fever, nausea, severe headache\n   Contradicting/absent: chills, splenomegaly, rigors, cyclic fever\n   Recommended tests: Thick and Thin Blood Smears with Giemsa Stain (trophozoites, Sch\u00fcffner dots, enlarged RBCs), Malaria Rapid Diagnostic Test (RDT with pan-LDH / aldolase target), G6PD Quantitative Enzyme Assay (mandatory before prescribing Primaquine/Tafenoquine), Giemsa-stained Thick and Thin Blood Films (serial q12h if initially negative), Malaria PCR (species identification: confirms P. vivax vs P. falciparum co-infection)\n\n3. Dengue Hemorrhagic Fever (Secondary Differential \u2014 98% Match)\n   Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; incubation period fits (10 days); 5 symptoms match; syndrome: neurological_fever\n   Supporting: fever, headache, intermittent fever, rapid heart rate, severe headache\n   Contradicting/absent: bleeding\n   Recommended tests: Serial Hematocrit & Platelet Count (q6-12h), Dengue NS1 Antigen / IgM ELISA, Serum Albumin (assess vascular leakage), Serial Hematocrit and Platelet counts every 4-6 hours during critical phase, Dengue NS1 Antigen and IgM/IgG Serology\n\n4. Malaria (Plasmodium falciparum) (Rule Out Consideration \u2014 98% Match)\n   Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; incubation period fits (10 days); 8 symptoms match; syndrome: neurological_fever\n   Supporting: confusion, fever, headache, intermittent fever, nausea, rapid heart rate, severe headache, thrombocytopenia\n   Contradicting/absent: chills, splenomegaly, rigors, cyclic fever\n   Recommended tests: Stat Thick and Thin Peripheral Blood Smears (Giemsa stain), Malaria Rapid Diagnostic Test (Pf-HRP2 antigen), Complete Blood Count (Anemia, Thrombocytopenia), Stat Thick and T