cle weakness, progressive difficulty walking and climbing stairs\n\n### Diagnostic Investigations\n* **Arylsulfatase A** [ORDERED] (Pending) \u2014 Pending\n* **Total 24-Hour Urinary Sulfatide Excretion Measurement** [ORDERED] (Pending) \u2014 Pending\n* **Brain MRI with Contrast** [ORDERED] (Pending) \u2014 Pending\n\n### Clinical Encounter Timeline (Chronological)\n**08 Oct 2026, 04:46 AM | Arrival & Triage \u2014 Emergency Department / Patient Arrival**\n* Finding: Patient presented with: Progressive difficulty walking and climbing stairs, muscle weakness, and loss of previously acquired motor abilities. Triage Vitals: Vitals recorded at bedside.\n* Action: Triage vitals recorded; IV access established; bed assigned in Acute Care.\n* Response: Patient stabilized for physician evaluation.\n\n**08 Oct 2026, 04:46 AM | History & Examination \u2014 Initial History & Systemic Physical Examination**\n* Finding: Presenting symptoms documented: Acute symptoms documented. Social/exposure history reviewed.\n* Action: Comprehensive physical and neurological examination documented by attending clinician.\n* Response: Initial clinical impression formulated.\n\n**08 Oct 2026, 04:46 AM | Initial Assessment \u2014 AI Differential Synthesized (v1) \u2014 Decision Support**\n* Finding: Considered diagnoses: Metachromatic Leukodystrophy (MLD) (98% Match), Friedreich''s Ataxia (FRDA) (92% Match), Juvenile Huntington''s Disease (Westphal Variant) (88% Match). Primary consideration: Metachromatic Leukodystrophy (MLD).\n* Action: Targeted diagnostic workup formulated based on clinical criteria and incubation fit.\n* Response: Orders prepared for laboratory and diagnostic imaging.\n\n**08 Oct 2026, 04:46 AM | Investigations Ordered \u2014 Diagnostic Investigations Dispatched (3 tests)**\n* Finding: Orders: Arylsulfatase A, Total 24-Hour Urinary Sulfatide Excretion Measurement, Brain MRI with Contrast.\n* Action: Specimens collected under aseptic precautions and requisitions transmitted.\n* Response: Awaiting laboratory processing and imaging execution.\n\n**08 Oct 2026, 04:46 AM | Treatment & Management \u2014 Empiric Management & Supportive Care Initiated**\n* Finding: Initial management directed towards acute presentation: Atidarsagene autotemcel (Lenmeldy gene therapy) or HSCT in eligible candidates; aggressive spasticity control (Baclofen).\n* Action: Administered IV fluids, symptomatic antipyretics, and targeted supportive measures.\n* Response: Tolerating medical therapy; continuous bedside monitoring active.\n\n**08 Oct 2026, 04:46 AM | Clinical Reassessment \u2014 Encounter Reassessment & Monitoring**\n* Finding: Hemodynamic parameters and neurological status monitored.\n* Action: Supportive care maintained; pending investigations tracked.\n* Response: Patient under continuous inpatient clinical care.\n\n**08 Oct 2026, 04:46 AM | Disposition \u2014 Clinician Disposition & Plan**\n* Finding: Inpatient care indicated for completion of diagnostic workup and clinical monitoring.\n* Action: Admitted to Ward / Unit; scheduled follow-up and safety net instructions established.\n* Response: Clinician authenticated.\n\n### Differential Diagnoses Considered (AI Decision Support)\n> *REFERENCE INFORMATION \u2014 CLINICIAN REVIEW REQUIRED \u2014 NOT A CONFIRMED DIAGNOSIS*\n\n1. **Metachromatic Leukodystrophy (MLD)** (Primary Consideration \u2014 98% Match)\n   * Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; 3 symptoms match\n   * Supporting evidence: difficulty walking, muscle weakness, weakness\n   * Contradicting / absent: progressive loss of coordination, speech may become difficult, tremor\n   * Recommended confirmation/exclusion: Arylsulfatase A (ARSA) Enzyme Activity Assay in Leukocytes or Cultured Fibroblasts (markedly deficient), Total 24-Hour Urinary Sulfatide Excretion Measurement (mass spectrometry), Brain MRI with Contrast (diffuse, symmetric T2/FLAIR hyperintensity in periventricular white matter with ''tigroid'' pattern), Leukocyte Arylsulfatase A Activity Assay, Urinary Sulfatides Quantification\n\n2. **Friedreich''s Ataxia (FRDA)** (Secondary Differential \u2014 92% Match)\n   * Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; 3 symptoms match\n   * Supporting evidence: difficulty walking, muscle weakness, weakness\n   * Contradicting / absent: problems with balance, progressive loss of coordination, speech may become difficult, loss of coordination\n   * Recommended confirmation/exclusion: FXN Gene Targeted Trinucleotide Repeat Expansion Analysis (detects homozygous GAA repeats in intron 1), Comprehensive Pediatric Neurological Examination (assess gait ataxia, Romberg sign, dysmetria, DTRs), Transthoracic Echocardiogram (TTE) & 12-Lead ECG (screen for concentric hypertrophic cardiomyopathy), FXN Molecular Genetic Repeat Expansion Testing, Transthoracic Echocardiogram (left ventricular wall thickness and ejection fraction)\n\n3. **Juvenile Huntington''s Disease (Westphal Variant)** (Secondary Differential \u2014 88% Match)\n   * Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; 3 symptoms match\n   * Supporting evidence: difficulty walking, muscle weakness, weakness\n   * Contradicting / absent: progressive loss of coordination, speech may become difficult, tremor, rigidity\n   * Recommended confirmation/exclusion: Targeted HTT Gene CAG Trinucleotide Repeat Sizing (detects expanded repeats >60 diagnostic of juvenile HD), High-Resolution Brain MRI (bilateral caudate nucleus and putamen atrophy with frontal horn dilatation), Standard Electroencephalogram (EEG) (evaluates frequent epileptic discharges and myoclonic seizures), HTT Gene CAG Repeat Sizing Assay, Brain MRI (caudate and striatal volumetric loss)\n\n4. **Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy (DMD)** (Rule Out Consideration \u2014 82% Match)\n   * Why considered: 3 symptoms match\n   * Supporting evidence: difficulty walking, muscle weakness, weakness\n   * Contradicting / absent: gowers sign, calf pseudohypertrophy, markedly elevated ck\n   * Recommended confirmation/exclusion: Serum Creatine Kinase (CK - markedly elevated >10,000\u201350,000 U/L), DMD Gene Multiplex Ligation-dependent Probe Amplification (MLPA) / Sequencing, Electromyography (EMG - small-amplitude, short-duration polyphasic motor unit potentials), Serum Creatine Kinase (massively elevated, 50-100x upper limit of normal), DMD Gene Genetic Testing: MLPA for exon deletions/duplications (70% of cases) followed by sequencing\n\n5. **Myasthenia Gravis & Myasthenic Crisis** (Rule Out Consideration \u2014 82% Match)\n   * Why considered: 3 symptoms match\n   * Supporting evidence: difficulty walking, muscle weakness, weakness\n   * Contradicting / absent: dysphagia (difficulty swallowing), diplopia (binocular double vision), bilateral asymmetric ptosis, slurred speech\n   * Recommended confirmation/exclusion: Bedside Ice Pack Test (apply ice pack to ptotic eyelid for 2 minutes; positive if ptosis improves by >=2 mm), Forced Vital Capacity (FVC) and Negative Inspiratory Force (NIF) \u2014 ''20/30/40 rule'': FVC <20 mL/kg, NIF < -30 cmH2O, or RR >40 indicates impending respiratory failure requiring elective intubation, Serum Acetylcholine Receptor (AChR) Binding, Blocking, and Modulating Antibodies (positive in 85% generalized MG), Repetitive Nerve Stimulation (RNS: >10% decremental compound muscle action potential at 2-3 Hz stimulation), Single-Fiber Electromyography (SFEMG \u2014 most sensitive diagnostic test; reveals increased jitter and impulse blocking)\n\n\n### Assessment & Plan\n**Clinician Assessment:** Under active clinical evaluation\n**Management Plan:**\nSupportive therapy and monitoring\n**Clinical Response / Hospital Course:** Patient under continuous observation\n**Disposition:** Inpatient Admission\n**Follow-Up:** Follow up in clinic\n**Safety Net Precautions:** Seek emergency evaluation if red flags develop\n\n### Clinician Authentication\n**Prepared by:** Department of General Medicine (General Medicine)\n**Date:** 08 October 2026 \u2014 04:50 AM\n**Authentication:** Electronic Authentication Verified"}}', 'draft', 1, true, '377df483-1a30-4a3c-9ae7-02f5799ad8a2', '2026-10-08 04:46:33.701935', '2026-10-08 04:46:33.701935', NULL, 'fa9e2981-e8cc-4a40-90a5-bbd5fb2303ae');
INSERT INTO public.clinician_feedback (suggestion_id, suggestion_type, doctor_id, decision, reason, suggestion_context, modified_context, model_version, knowledge_version, id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('inv-3ea4e6a6-3dcd-4bdc-a2a0-ae8d90770bf3-Pulmonary Embolism-Arterial Blood Gas (ABG)', 'investigation', '903c2fee-d0b7-405c-b627-9bdfa3bd2f51', 'ACCEPT', NULL, '{"name": "Arterial Blood Gas (ABG)", "priority": "HIGH PRIORITY", "rationale": "Detects acute hypoxemia, hypocapnia, respiratory alkalosis, and increased alveolar-arterial (A-a) oxygen gradient.", "relevant_clinical_finding": "Based on Pulmonary Embolism workup", "evidence": "American Thoracic Society Guidelines", "limitations": "", "safety_flags": [], "provenance": "offline_clinical_guideline_panel"}', 'null', '1.0.0', '1.0.0', '98007ebd-75d0-4b07-9180-ae6d1347c675', '2026-09-24 17:53:37.461653', '2026-09-24 17:53:37.461653');
INSERT INTO public.clinician_feedback (suggestion_id, suggestion_type, doctor_id, decision, reason, suggestion_context, modified_context, model_version, knowledge_version, id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('note-dd6d73ca-9a1b-4783-bbed-759dec17ea30-differential_diagnosis-3', 'clinical_note', '903c2fee-d0b7-405c-b627-9bdfa3bd2f51', 'REJECT', 'NOT RELEVENT', '{"section": "differential_diagnosis", "original_text": "1. Bundibugyo Virus Disease (BVD) (Primary Consideration \u2014 98% Match)\n   Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; endemic in visited countries (0.35 geographic score); incubation period fits (10 days); 5 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   Supporting: bleeding, diarrhea, fever, hematemesis, muscle aches\n   Contradicting/absent: vomiting, profound weakness, sore throat\n   Recommended tests: Stat Bundibugyo ebolavirus RT-PCR (Whole Blood \u2014 BSL-4 facility), Complete Blood Count with differential (marked lymphopenia, profound thrombocytopenia), Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (AST >> ALT, BUN, creatinine, electrolyte panel), Stat Bundibugyo ebolavirus RT-PCR (Blood \u2014 BSL-4 with BDBV-specific primers), Serial Complete Blood Count (monitoring thrombocytopenia and hemoconcentration)\n\n2. Ebola Virus Disease (Zaire) (Secondary Differential \u2014 98% Match)\n   Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; endemic in visited countries (0.35 geographic score); incubation period fits (10 days); 6 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   Supporting: bleeding, diarrhea, fever, hematemesis, muscle aches, rash\n   Contradicting/absent: weakness, vomiting\n   Recommended tests: Stat Filovirus RT-PCR (Blood \u2014 BSL-4 facility), Complete Blood Count (Leukopenia, marked Thrombocytopenia), Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (LFTs with AST >> ALT, Creatinine, BUN), Stat Filovirus RT-PCR (BSL-4 reference lab), Serial CBC with differential and Platelets\n\n3. Dengue Hemorrhagic Fever (Secondary Differential \u2014 98% Match)\n   Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; incubation period fits (10 days); 5 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   Supporting: bleeding, fever, hemorrhage, muscle aches, rash\n   Contradicting/absent: severe headache\n   Recommended tests: Serial Hematocrit & Platelet Count (q6-12h), Dengue NS1 Antigen / IgM ELISA, Serum Albumin (assess vascular leakage), Serial Hematocrit and Platelet counts every 4-6 hours during critical phase, Dengue NS1 Antigen and IgM/IgG Serology\n\n4. Marburg Virus Disease (Rule Out Consideration \u2014 98% Match)\n   Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; endemic in visited countries (0.35 geographic score); incubation period fits (10 days); 5 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   Supporting: bleeding, diarrhea, fever, muscle aches, rash\n   Contradicting/absent: weakness, severe headache\n   Recommended tests: Stat Marburg RT-PCR (Blood \u2014 BSL-4), Complete Blood Count with differential, Renal & Liver Function Panels, Stat Marburg virus RT-PCR (Blood - BSL-4), Complete Blood Count and Platelet Count\n\n5. Acute Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding (Rule Out Consideration \u2014 98% Match)\n   Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; 1 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   Supporting: hematemesis\n   Contradicting/absent: coffee ground emesis, melena, hypotension\n   Recommended tests: Two Large-Bore Peripheral IV Lines (16- or 18-gauge) or Rapid Infuser Catheter for aggressive crystalloid/blood resuscitation, STAT Type and Screen with Crossmatch for 4 to 6 units Packed Red Blood Cells (PRBCs) and Plasma, Complete Blood Count with Platelets (initial hematocrit may be falsely normal before volume equilibration), Urgent Esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD: within 24 hours of presentation, or within 12 hours if hemodynamically unstable or suspected varices), Serial Hemoglobin / Hematocrit every 4 to 6 hours to assess ongoing hemorrhage", "edited_text": "1. Bundibugyo Virus Disease (BVD) (Primary Consideration \u2014 98% Match)\n   Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; endemic in visited countries (0.35 geographic score); incubation period fits (10 days); 5 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   Supporting: bleeding, diarrhea, fever, hematemesis, muscle aches\n   Contradicting/absent: vomiting, profound weakness, sore throat\n   Recommended tests: Stat Bundibugyo ebolavirus RT-PCR (Whole Blood \u2014 BSL-4 facility), Complete Blood Count with differential (marked lymphopenia, profound thrombocytopenia), Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (AST >> ALT, BUN, creatinine, electrolyte panel), Stat Bundibugyo ebolavirus RT-PCR (Blood \u2014 BSL-4 with BDBV-specific primers), Serial Complete Blood Count (monitoring thrombocytopenia and hemoconcentration)\n\n2. Ebola Virus Disease (Zaire) (Secondary Differential \u2014 98% Match)\n   Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; endemic in visited countries (0.35 geographic score); incubation period fits (10 days); 6 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   Supporting: bleeding, diarrhea, fever, hematemesis, muscle aches, rash\n   Contradicting/absent: weakness, vomiting\n   Recommended tests: Stat Filovirus RT-PCR (Blood \u2014 BSL-4 facility), Complete Blood Count (Leukopenia, marked Thrombocytopenia), Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (LFTs with AST >> ALT, Creatinine, BUN), Stat Filovirus RT-PCR (BSL-4 reference lab), Serial CBC with differential and Platelets\n\n3. Dengue Hemorrhagic Fever (Secondary Differential \u2014 98% Match)\n   Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; incubation period fits (10 days); 5 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   Supporting: bleeding, fever, hemorrhage, muscle aches, rash\n   Contradicting/absent: severe headache\n   Recommended tests: Serial Hematocrit & Platelet Count (q6-12h), Dengue NS1 Antigen / IgM ELISA, Serum Albumin (assess vascular leakage), Serial Hematocrit and Platelet counts every 4-6 hours during critical phase, Dengue NS1 Antigen and IgM/IgG Serology\n\n4. Marburg Virus Disease (Rule Out Consideration \u2014 98% Match)\n   Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; endemic in visited countries (0.35 geographic score); incubation period fits (10 days); 5 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   Supporting: bleeding, diarrhea, fever, muscle aches, rash\n   Contradicting/absent: weakness, severe headache\n   Recommended tests: Stat Marburg RT-PCR (Blood \u2014 BSL-4), Complete Blood Count with differential, Renal & Liver Function Panels, Stat Marburg virus RT-PCR (Blood - BSL-4), Complete Blood Count and Platelet Count\n\n5. Acute Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding (Rule Out Consideration \u2014 98% Match)\n   Why considered: hallmark clinical signs present; 1 symptoms match; syndrome: rash_fever, zoonotic\n   Supporting: hematemesis\n   Contradicting/absent: coffee ground emesis, melena, hypotension\n   Recommended tests: Two Large-Bore Peripheral IV Lines (16- or 18-gauge) or Rapid Infuser Catheter for aggressive crystalloid/blood resuscitation, STAT Type and Screen with Crossmatch for 4 to 6 units Packed Red Blood Cells (PRBCs) and Plasma, Complete Blood Count with Platelets (initial hematocrit may be falsely normal before volume equilibration), Urgent Esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD: within 24 hours of presentation, or within 12 hours if hemodynamically unstable or suspected varices), Serial Hemoglobin / Hematocrit every 4 to 6 hours to assess ongoing hemorrhage"}', 'null', '1.0.0', '1.0.0', '834f00d2-7931-4c49-80b1-ec7bf7e7d3d5', '2026-10-04 14:16:19.120376', '2026-10-04 14:16:19.120376');
